 Data Imputation (Filling Missing Values)

The selected operation is Data Imputation, specifically filling missing values in the 'act_dep_time' column. This will be done using a simple method such as Mean Imputation or Median Imputation for numerical columns like this one.

Mean Imputation involves replacing missing values with the mean of the non-missing values in the same column. While this method is easy to implement, it may not always accurately represent the data if there are extreme outliers.

Median Imputation, on the other hand, replaces missing values with the median of the non-missing values in the same column. This method is less affected by extreme outliers and is generally more robust than mean imputation.

Both methods can be used to fill missing values, but it's essential to understand their strengths and weaknesses before choosing one for a specific dataset. In this case, since there are no apparent extreme outliers in the 'act_dep_time' column, either method could work well. However, median imputation might be more suitable due to its robustness against outliers.

